4c:["$","$L6f",null,{"type":"multiple","children":[["$","$L70",null,{"title":"Can Prometheus scrape Forge directly?","id":"can-prometheus-scrape-forge-directly","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["No. The binary has no scrape endpoint, at any configuration, and\nthe binary cannot push to Prometheus's OTLP receiver either, because that\nreceiver is HTTP-only and Forge's exporters are gRPC-only. Run a\ncollector and scrape its Prometheus exporter; the shipped collector also\nforwards into Prometheus's OTLP receiver, with the renaming described in\n",["$","$L8",null,{"href":"#what-happens-if-you-skip-the-collector","children":"what happens if you skip the collector"}],"."]}]}],["$","$L70",null,{"title":"Do metrics require a commercial licence?","id":"do-metrics-require-a-commercial-licence","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["Metrics are one of the Business Source Licensed modules. Individual\ndeveloper use is fine; shared, production or commercial settings need\nForge Enterprise or a separate licence; see the\n",["$","$L8",null,{"href":"https://forge.com/license","children":"licence page"}],". No configuration\nturns instrumentation off, which\n",["$","$L8",null,{"href":"/reference/oss-and-enterprise#metrics-are-always-on","children":"Open source and Enterprise"}],"\nexplains and which matters when you assess your own position."]}]}],["$","$L70",null,{"title":"Which metric should drive worker autoscaling?","id":"which-metric-should-drive-worker-autoscaling","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["Queue time, not queue depth. ",["$","code",null,{"children":"forge_execution_queue_time"}]," is a real\nhistogram emitted by the scheduler; a p95 above your tolerance means add\nworkers. ",["$","code",null,{"children":"forge_scheduler_queue_depth"}]," is a gauge of what the last\nmatching pass read, ",["$","code",null,{"children":"forge_execution_active_count{execution_stage=\"queued\"}"}],"\ncounts the same thing from the other side, and ",["$","code",null,{"children":"forge_worker_connected_count"}],"\ntells you how many workers that queue is waiting on. Before you build an HPA on either, read the\n",["$","$L8",null,{"href":"/operate/autoscaling-reference#the-reaction-time-budget","children":"autoscaling reference"}],":\nthe default one-minute export interval alone puts your reaction time in\nminutes, and CPU-based scaling is worse because workers are mostly I/O-bound."]}]}],["$","$L70",null,{"title":"Why does my dashboard show a new instance every restart?","id":"why-does-my-dashboard-show-a-new-instance-every-restart","children":["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","code",null,{"children":"service.instance.id"}]," is a fresh UUIDv4 per process and ",["$","code",null,{"children":"OTEL_SERVICE_NAME"}],"\nis ignored. Set a stable identity of your own through\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES"}],", for example ",["$","code",null,{"children":"forge.instance_name"}],", and\naggregate on that. The shipped collector config copies that attribute onto\nevery datapoint as ",["$","code",null,{"children":"instance_name"}],", which is the label the shipped\nrecording rules group by; nothing in the binary sets it for you."]}]}]]}]
